            <ArticleTitle>Tabetic lightning pains: high-dosage intravenous penicillin versus carbamazepine therapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>424-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The relief of tabetic lightning pains (LP) by carbamazepine (CBZ) was questioned as in some instances penicillin was given simultaneously. Only 1 out of 6 cases became pain-free on high-dosage intravenous penicillin, while CBZ rapidly abolished LP in those unresponsive to penicillin. Gastric crises were similarly abated by CBZ in 1 case under a placebo-controlled study. Inflammatory and degenerative changes in the posterior nerve root and ganglia may be the underlying pathologic basis that explains these two different responses to penicillin.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
